---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import Navigation from '../../components/navbar.astro';

// Base URL for GitHub Pages
const baseUrl = '/serra2';

// Page metadata
const pageTitle = "The Genie-Blessed Angel - Serra 2.0";
const pageDescription = "Understanding the foundational principle behind Serra Angel and the Serra 2.0 project";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="description" content={pageDescription}>
  <title>{pageTitle}</title>
</head>

<body>
  <Navigation currentPage="about" />

  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>The Genie-Blessed Angel</h1>
  </header>

  <main class="container">
    <article class="about-article">
      
      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          The curation of this exhibition sparked rich discussions within the community. Jense Gerrits, 
          from the Netherlands, has kindly loaned us the singular international edition Serra Angel from 
          his private collection, autographed by Douglas Shuler. On this specific piece, Shuler hand-drew 
          the lamp of a "good genie", a fascinating creative nod, considering that the original artwork 
          for the Aladdin's Lamp card (from the Arabian Nights expansion) was 
          actually created by Mark Tedin.
          By merging these two worlds, Shuler crafted a hybrid work in which 
          the smoke rising from the lamp appears to give birth to the Angel. To 
          highlight this one-of-a-kind specimen, I chose to frame it alongside a 
          print by Alexandre Monteiro, better known as Hopare. A prominent 
          figure in the Parisian urban art scene and represented by Galerie 
          Bayard (Paris), Hopare's "half-angel, half-genie" portrait provides a 
          powerful contemporary backdrop to Shuler's illustration. It invites us 
          to wonder: could the Angel's legendary power be the ultimate wish 
          that every player hoped a genie would grant ?
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="article-image2">
        <img src=`${baseUrl}/articles/aladdin.jpg` alt="Serra Angel missprint from the Spanish version of Fourth Edition">
      </div>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h3>A Bit of History… Aladdin's Lamp</h3> 
        <p>
          Upon the release of the Arabian Nights
          expansion in December 1993, Aladdin's 
          Lamp became the card with the highest 
          mana value in the game. It dethroned 
          heavyweights like Force of Nature and Lord 
          of the Pit, which cost "only" 6 or 7 mana at 
          the time. The Lamp held this record until 
          the arrival of Polar Kraken in the Ice Age set in June 1995. 
          In the original Arabian Nights printing, the mana cost is displayed as two gray circles, 
          each containing the number 5. This was because "mana bubbles" for double-digit 
          numbers didn't exist yet in the game's graphic design. Old school Magic was truly wild! 
        </p>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>&copy; 2026 Serra 2.0 Project. All rights reserved.</p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>